<chapter
id="datatypes"
>#title("Datatypes")

<!-- ****** ****** -->

#para('\

Datatypes are a form of user-defined types for classifying data (or
values). The support for datatypes and pattern matching in ATS is primarily
adopted from ML. In this dialect of ATS2, which has no garbage collector
(GC), there are two kinds of them: a #keycode("datatype") has only nullary
data constructors (that is, it is an enumeration), and a
#keycode("datavtype") is a #emphasis("linear") datatype whose values are
allocated on heap and have to be freed by the programmer. The compiler
rejects a #keycode("datatype") with a non-nullary constructor, because
the values of such a type could only be reclaimed by GC:

<programlisting>
error(2): nonlinear heap allocation is not supported (no GC): the datatype
[intlst] has a non-nullary constructor; use [datavtype] instead.
</programlisting>

')

#para('\

The following code declares a datatype of the name #stacode("weekday") for
values representing weekdays:

#sats2xhtml('\
datatype weekday =
  | Monday | Tuesday | Wednesday | Thursday | Friday
')

There are five data constructors associated with #stacode("weekday"), which
are
#dyncode("Monday"),
#dyncode("Tuesday"),
#dyncode("Wednesday"),
#dyncode("Thursday"), and
#dyncode("Friday").
All of these data constructors are nullary,
that is, they take no arguments to
form values (of the type #stacode("weekday")).\

')

#para('\

Each nullary constructor is represented as a small integer
(e.g., one that is less than 1024). One can use the following
function #dyncode("weekday2int") to find out the integers reprsenting
the constructors associated with #stacode("weekday"):

#dats2xhtml('\
staload UN = $UNSAFE

(* ****** ****** *)
//
datatype weekday =
  | Monday | Tuesday | Wednesday | Thursday | Friday
//
(* ****** ****** *)
//
fun
weekday2int
  (wd: weekday): int = $UN.cast{int}($UN.cast{intptr}(wd))
')

The small integer representing a nullary constructor is often referred to
as the tag of the constructor.

In this case,
the tags for
#dyncode("Monday"),
#dyncode("Tuesday"),
#dyncode("Wednesday"),
#dyncode("Thursday"), and
#dyncode("Friday")
are 0, 1, 2, 3, and 4, respectively.
')

#para('\

Given a nullary constructor #dyncode("foo"), both #dyncode("foo") and
#dyncode("foo()") can be used to refer the value constructed by
#dyncode("foo").  However, only #dyncode("foo()") can be used as the
pattern that matches this value. For instance, the following function tests
whether a given value of the type #stacode("weekday") is formed with the
constructor #dyncode("Friday"):

#dats2xhtml('\
fun
isFriday(x: weekday): bool =
  case+ x of Friday() => true | _ => false
')

Note that the pattern #dyncode("Friday()") cannot be replaced
with #dyncode("Friday") as the latter is treated as a variable when
used as a pattern. On the other hand, both of the following
assertions hold at run-time as #dyncode("Friday") and #dyncode("Friday()")
refer to the same value:

#dats2xhtml('\
val () = assertloc (isFriday(Friday))
val () = assertloc (isFriday(Friday()))
')

Values of such a type are not allocated on heap, so there is nothing to
free.

')

#para('\

If there is only one constructor associated with a datavtype, then
there is no tag in the representation for values of this datavtype.

')

#para('\

All the other datatypes of this chapter allocate their values on heap, so
they are declared with the keyword #keycode("datavtype"). A value of a
datavtype is linear: it can be consumed only once, and the compiler
reports an error if a value is dropped or used again. A function either
borrows a value (the parameter is annotated with #keycode("!")) or
consumes it (it frees the value or returns it). A value is freed by a
pattern-matching that uses the symbol #keycode("~") in front of the
constructor, as is shown below.

')

#para('\

A datavtype is list-like if there are two data constructors associated with
it such that one is nullary (nil-like) and the other is non-nullary
(cons-like). For instance, the datavtype #stacode("ab") declared as follows
is list-like:

#sats2xhtml('\
datavtype ab = AA of () | BB of (bool)
')

The values of a list-like datavtype are represented in a special way.  Given
a value of the datavtype; if it is constructed with the nil-like
constructor, then it is represented as the null-pointer; if it is
constructed with the cons-like constructor, then it is reprenstend as a
heap-allocated tuple containing all of the arguments passed to the
constructor.

In the above case, the value #dyncode("AA()") is represented as the null
pointer, and the value #dyncode("BB(b)") for each boolean #dyncode("b") is
represented as a heap-allocated singleton tuple containing the only
component #dyncode("b"). This description can be readily verified with
the following code (the function #dyncode("ab_free") frees a value of the
type #stacode("ab")):

#dats2xhtml('\
fun
ab_free(x: ab): void = case+ x of ~AA() => () | ~BB(_) => ()

val () = let
  val x = AA()
  val () = assertloc (iseqz($UN.castvwtp1{ptr}(x)))
in
  ab_free(x)

val () = let
  val x = BB(true)
  val () = assertloc (true = $UN.ptr0_get<bool>($UN.castvwtp1{ptr}(x)))
in
  ab_free(x)
')

')

#para('\

The following declaration introduces a datavtype of the name #stacode("abc"):

#sats2xhtml('\
datavtype abc =
  | A of () | B of (bool) | C of (int, double)
')

The three constructors associated with #stacode("abc") are #dyncode("A"),
#dyncode("B"), and #dyncode("C"); #dyncode("A") is nullary; #dyncode("B")
is unary, taking a boolean to form a value (of the type #stacode("abc"));
#dyncode("C") is binary, taking an integer and a float (of double precision)
to form a value (of the type #stacode("abc")).

')

#para('\

In a general case, if a data constructor is n-ary for some positive n, then
each value it constructs is a heap-allocated tuple of n+1 components, where
the first one is the tag assigned to the constructor and the rest are the
arguments passed to the constructor.  For instance, the following function
can be called to find out the tags assigned to the constructors associated
with #stacode("abc") (the argument is borrowed, so the function does not
consume it), and the function #dyncode("abc_free") frees a value:

#dats2xhtml('\
fun
abc2tag
(x: !abc): int = let
  val p = $UN.castvwtp1{intptr}(x)
in
//
case+ 0 of
| _ when p < 1024 => $UN.cast{int}(p)
| _ (*heap-allocated*) => $UN.ptr0_get<int>($UN.cast{ptr}(p))
//
end // end of [abc2tag]

fun
abc_free(x: abc): void =
  case+ x of ~A() => () | ~B(_) => () | ~C(_, _) => ()

')

In this case, the tags assigned to #dyncode("A"), #dyncode("B"), and
#dyncode("C") are 0, 1, and 2, respectively.

')

#para('\

Datavtypes can be defined recursively. As an example,
the following declaration introduces a recursively defined datavtype
#stacode("exp") (for representing arithemetic integer expressions):

#sats2xhtml('\
datavtype exp =
| Int of int
| Neg of (exp)
| Add of (exp, exp)
| Sub of (exp, exp)
')

For instance, #dyncode("(1+2)-3")
can be represented as #dyncode("Sub(Add(Int(1), Int(2)), Int(3))").
As another example, the following code introduces two mutually recursively
defined datavtypes #stacode("intexp") and #stacode("boolexp")
(for integer expressions and boolean expressions, respectively):

#sats2xhtml('\
datavtype
intexp =
| IInt of int
| INeg of (intexp)
| IAdd of (intexp, intexp)
| ISub of (intexp, intexp)
| IfThenElse of (boolexp, intexp, intexp)
and
boolexp =
| Bool of bool
| Not of (boolexp)
| Disj of (boolexp, boolexp)
| Conj of (boolexp, boolexp)
| Less of (intexp, intexp)
| LessEq of (intexp, intexp)
')

The code below implements two mutually recursive functions
#dyncode("eval_intexp") and #dyncode("eval_boolexp") for evaluating integer
expressions and boolean expressions, respectively. The expression to
evaluate is only borrowed (its type is preceded by #keycode("!")):

#dats2xhtml('\
symintr eval
//
extern
fun eval_intexp : (!intexp) -> int
extern
fun eval_boolexp : (!boolexp) -> bool
//
overload eval with eval_intexp
overload eval with eval_boolexp
//
extern
fun free_intexp : (intexp) -> void
extern
fun free_boolexp : (boolexp) -> void

implement
eval_intexp
  (e0) = (
//
case+ e0 of
| IInt (i) => i
| INeg (e) => ~eval(e)
| IAdd (e1, e2) => eval(e1) + eval(e2)
| ISub (e1, e2) => eval(e1) - eval(e2)
| IfThenElse
    (e_test, e_then, e_else) =>
    if eval(e_test) then eval(e_then) else eval(e_else)
//
) (* end of [eval_intexp] *)
//
implement
eval_boolexp
  (e0) = (
//
case+ e0 of
| Bool (b) => b
| Not (e) => ~eval(e)
| Less (e1, e2) => eval(e1) < eval(e2)
| LessEq (e1, e2) => eval(e1) <= eval(e2)
| Conj (e1, e2) => eval(e1) && eval(e2)
| Disj (e1, e2) => eval(e1) || eval(e2)
//
) (* end of [eval_boolexp] *)
')

Since the expressions are linear, they have to be freed once they are of no
more use. The following two mutually recursive functions free an integer
expression and a boolean expression, respectively (the symbol
#keycode("~") in a pattern means that the matched value is freed):

#dats2xhtml('\
implement
free_intexp
  (e0) = (
//
case+ e0 of
| ~IInt (_) => ()
| ~INeg (e) => free_intexp(e)
| ~IAdd (e1, e2) => (free_intexp(e1); free_intexp(e2))
| ~ISub (e1, e2) => (free_intexp(e1); free_intexp(e2))
| ~IfThenElse
    (e_test, e_then, e_else) =>
    (free_boolexp(e_test); free_intexp(e_then); free_intexp(e_else))
//
) (* end of [free_intexp] *)
//
implement
free_boolexp
  (e0) = (
//
case+ e0 of
| ~Bool (_) => ()
| ~Not (e) => free_boolexp(e)
| ~Less (e1, e2) => (free_intexp(e1); free_intexp(e2))
| ~LessEq (e1, e2) => (free_intexp(e1); free_intexp(e2))
| ~Conj (e1, e2) => (free_boolexp(e1); free_boolexp(e2))
| ~Disj (e1, e2) => (free_boolexp(e1); free_boolexp(e2))
//
) (* end of [free_boolexp] *)
')

Here is an example of use:

#dats2xhtml('\
val E0 =
IfThenElse (
  Less(IInt(1), IInt(2)), ISub(IInt(11), IInt(1)), IAdd(IInt(12), IInt(13))
) (* end of [val] *)

val () = assertloc (eval(E0) = 10)
val () = free_intexp (E0)
')

If the last line is deleted, the compiler complains that the linear
variable #dyncode("E0") is not consumed. The datavtypes presented in this
chapter are simple datavtypes.
Other more advanced forms of datatypes include polymorphic datatypes
(such as the linear lists #stacode("list_vt")),
dependent datatypes, which will be covered elsewhere.

')

